 # Implementation handoff
 
-## Compass verification, 2026-11-12
+## Compass verification, 2026-11-17
+
+Further two-wearable testing found that ESP-NOW packets arrive and can be paired with headings. During an unrestricted turn, gyro-based gravity was unavailable for long periods; during a level turn, headings remained mostly valid, but direction confidence appeared only briefly. The user's compass breakout is mounted vertically on its edge while the display faces up. Current firmware assumes magnetometer and IMU axes are aligned, so the physical axis mapping must be established before relying on tilt compensation or direction. The temporary diagnostic firmware was replaced with the normal build. Signal contrast also often fell below the existing 6 dB confidence gate; do not relax it without checking direction accuracy.
+
+After the user fixed the compass modules in place and calibrated them, both wearables still intermittently showed NO HEADING. A recorded level turn reproduced the dropout: 307 of 702 frames lacked a gravity estimate. GravityEstimator now predicts gravity from the gyro after its quiet settling window and bounds prediction without a matching accelerometer correction to 250 ms. The recorded level-turn regression now reports 22 unavailable frames. Both wearables were flashed; a repeated level-turn test on port ending 1891 reported 130 of 135 headings valid, and the user reported both stationary peer screens showed TURN TO SAMPLE. Calibrations were preserved. Heading accuracy while worn and acquisition of the green peer marker remain to be checked. Send serial `t` to identify a wearable with a jump reaction before asking the user to manipulate it.
+
+## Earlier compass investigation, 2026-11-12
 
 Both peer-direction screens alternated between NO HEADING and TURN TO SAMPLE. A temporary diagnostic on port ending 1891 showed successful magnetometer reads but frequent zero gravity estimates during a controlled, flat turn; tilt rotation repeatedly crossed the 10-degree/second gate and restarted the 250 ms settling window. With the wearable stationary, three consecutive two-second windows reported valid headings throughout. The normal firmware was restored after testing. Investigate motion handling without weakening the rejection of unreliable gravity; peer-direction sampling remains unverified. To identify a wearable for the user, send serial `t` to trigger its jump reaction before asking them to move or inspect it.
 
-The normal-turn trace is `tests/fixtures/2026-11-12-normal-turn.txt`. Each line is `nowMs sensorTimestamp axMg ayMg azMg gxDps gyDps gzDps recordedFlag`. `sh tools/replay_gravity_trace.sh` replays that file through `GravityEstimator` and reports 347 of 478 samples without gravity (exit 1). The trailing flag is from the capture and is ignored. Tilt rate exceeded 10 degrees/second in 188 samples; 13 acceleration magnitudes were outside the accepted 0.9–1.1 g range. Some tilt rates were much higher than the threshold. The diagnostic firmware was removed from the wearable. The bias and tilt-average experiments below were offline checks of this trace; the replay tool does not repeat them.
+The normal-turn trace is `tests/fixtures/2026-11-12-normal-turn.txt`. Each line is `nowMs sensorTimestamp axMg ayMg azMg gxDps gyDps gzDps recordedFlag`. Before the 2026-11-17 fix, replaying it reported 347 of 478 samples without gravity (exit 1). The trailing flag is from the capture and is ignored. Tilt rate exceeded 10 degrees/second in 188 samples; 13 acceleration magnitudes were outside the accepted 0.9–1.1 g range. Some tilt rates were much higher than the threshold. The diagnostic firmware was removed from the wearable. The bias and tilt-average experiments below were offline checks of this trace; the replay tool does not repeat them.
 
 The final 96 recorded samples, after the user set the wearable down, averaged gyro X=5.27, Y=-6.18, Z=-0.01 degrees/second (standard deviations 0.62, 0.65, 0.71). Subtracting an approximate fixed bias (5, -6, 0) for an offline experiment reduced unavailable gravity from 347/478 to 310/478; gyro offset contributes but does not explain the whole failure. A two- to eight-sample tilt-rate average also left at least 66.5% unavailable. Neither is a sufficient fix; avoid hard-coding this board's bias or weakening motion rejection based on this trace alone.
 
